-- Skill: net-maker
-- Description: Make a network or a skill from a NeuroLang program, see what each weight does, change it a little, and compile code into weights.
-- Written by hand, not generated by SkillMakerExtension. The program statements are in wiki/Net-Maker.md.

name="net-maker_perceive"
name="net-maker_plan"
name="net-maker_build"
name="net-maker_inspect"
name="net-maker_edit"
name="net-maker_respond"

"net-maker_perceive"@definition="Read what is wanted: a network of its own (kind network, the net maker) or something that changes the AI it joins (kind skill, the net skill maker). A skill grafts into the live AI, so say so and ask before making one."
"net-maker_plan"@definition="Decide the program: how many neurons, which neuron is typed into (the input), which neuron answers (the output), what each input should make the output say (response), which neurons are frozen now or after training, and how to train: responses, predict given text, or random."
"net-maker_build"@definition="Make it with POST /api/nets, or netMaker and netSkillMaker in code. Report what came back: whether every response was met, how many land on their own response, what was skipped because its neuron is frozen, and for predict or random the accuracy beside the commonest-byte baseline and the share of held-out text already seen in training."
"net-maker_inspect"@definition="To see what a weight does, type something that highlights a neuron and say what the output should be (POST /api/nets/:id/view). Read which weights carry the output, which way each pushes, and which way each should move. The one-step neighbour terms are read only."
"net-maker_edit"@definition="Change the net a little and go on: nudge one weight (at most 0.1), or take a small step that keeps its changes only if the output got closer to the goal, and keep going while it helps. Every change is logged and can be undone. A frozen neuron cannot be edited. Say how far the output moved, not that it is fixed."
"net-maker_respond"@definition="Tell the owner plainly what was made and what the numbers say, including what did not work. To turn code into weights use the compiler (POST /api/compile): straight-line arithmetic and polynomials compile exactly, other numeric functions are fitted, anything else is only recognised."

"net-maker_perceive"@role="input"
"net-maker_respond"@role="output"

"net-maker_perceive"@connections=".net-maker_plan*0.8"
"net-maker_plan"@connections=".net-maker_build*0.8"
"net-maker_build"@connections=".net-maker_inspect*0.8"
"net-maker_inspect"@connections=".net-maker_edit*0.8"
"net-maker_edit"@connections=".net-maker_respond*0.8"
